\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Scalar frames for an orbit field}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{Use the \vocabk{orbit gauge–Higgs weight}{fdip-010A} over the
commutative unital ring #{k}. Write #{G^\circ} for the stabilizer
of the reference scalar #{\phi^\circ\in\mathcal O}. An
\newvocab{orbit section} is a function #{\orbitlift :\mathcal O\to G}
satisfying
##{\orbitlift(o)\phi^\circ=o,\qquad \orbitlift(\phi^\circ)=1.}
It exists by choosing a transporter for each point of the finite
orbit and the identity at the reference point. A
\newvocab{scalar frame} at #{x} is the transporter
#{g_x=\orbitlift(\phi_x)}. In these frames the
\newvocab{reduced link} from #{s(e)} to #{h(e)} is
##{\check U_e=g_{h(e)}^{-1}U_e g_{s(e)}.}
When the orbit is free, #{G^\circ=\{1\}} and each transporter
is unique. Indeed, two transporters to the same point differ on
the right by a member of #{G^\circ}.}
